  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM EDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nThis afternoon GOES water vapor imagery and GFS 500mb model height \nanalysis showed zonal flow in place for the northern tier of the \nU.S. with the exception of the Pacific Northwest where a trough was \nbeginning to dig south over the adjacent waters of the Pacific. \nOverall it was a pleasant afternoon for the U.P. with high pressure \nin control leading to temperatures warming above average, into the \nupper 50s to low 60s. Skies were mainly sunny and winds were much \nlighter than the last few days, mainly from the northeast 10 mph or \nless.\n\nMainly zonal flow is expected over the next few days with continued \nunseasonably warm afternoon temperatures in the 60s (10-15F above \naverage) along with dry conditions and relatively light winds. The \nupper level low pressure near the Pacific Northwest will continue to \ndig south and cut off from the main flow, moving onshore over \nCalifornia later this week. It will then transition to an open wave \nand lift northeast through the intermountain west. Downstream \nridging will amplify over the Midwest helping to maintain the warm \nand dry weather across the U.P. through the end of the work week. \nThereafter the low will lift into the Northern Plains and into \nOntario this weekend. Ahead of the system southerly winds will \nincrease leading to another warm and windy weekend. This could lead \nto elevated fire weather conditions, particularly on Sunday when \npost-frontal west-southwest winds and subsidence will combine for \ndrier and windy conditions. However, details are still murky at this \ntime and probabilities for critical fire weather conditions remain \nlow, less than 15%. With the front there will be a chance for \nshowers (30% chance) this weekend but moisture is very limited and \nwidespread, appreciable rainfall is not expected.",
